Usages:
  composition_root: |
    main() is a flag-driven composition root: it initializes remotewrite (relabeling, stream
    aggregation, per-remote-URL queues) before promscrape, then wires promscrape's scrape
    results directly into remotewrite via a callback parameter
    (promscrape.Init(remotewrite.PushDropSamplesOnFailure)) rather than through a separate
    "start scraping" step. Roughly 30 HTTP routes are dispatched by one large requestHandler
    switch to ~16 sibling protocol subpackages (the same push-protocol family app/vminsert
    supports: promremotewrite, influx, graphite, opentsdb, opentelemetry, newrelic, datadogv1/v2,
    zabbixconnector, etc.), each ultimately calling into remotewrite's own push path rather than
    a storage cell directly — vmagent never touches lib/storage; it only ever forwards to a
    remote_write-compatible endpoint (which may itself be a vminsert/vmstorage instance).

Annotations: |
  This cell is the edge-agent binary: it scrapes/receives metrics from many sources and forwards
  them, relabeled and optionally stream-aggregated, to a remote_write-compatible storage system.
  It directly imports and drives the lifecycle of lib/promscrape (scrape config validation,
  start, stop, status/debug endpoints); relabeling of forwarded samples happens one level below
  this cell, inside its own remotewrite subpackage (which is where the real
  lib/promrelabel dependency lives — out of this cell's own directory scope, since the location
  directive cannot traverse into subpackages), described here in prose per this forest's
  Dependency Map rule.

  Use `composition_root` for the real initialization order and request-routing shape.

---

"main()":
  location: main.go
  annotations: |
    Parse flags, initialize every ingestion/forwarding subsystem in dependency order, serve
    HTTP until terminated, then shut everything down in reverse order.

    Algorithm:
    1. Tune the garbage collector for low steady-state memory use
    2. Parse flags and initialize logging/build-info/OpenTelemetry/per-series limits
    3. If a dry-run flag is set, validate the scrape config and relabel/stream-aggregation
       configs, then exit without starting anything
    4. Start the remote-write pipeline (relabeling, stream aggregation, ingestion-rate limiting,
       per-remote-URL queues) — see `composition_root`
    5. Start a pool of parser worker goroutines, then any configured raw Graphite/Influx/OpenTSDB
       TCP or UDP listeners
    6. Start the scrape subsystem, wired to push scraped samples into the remote-write pipeline
       started in step 4
    7. Serve HTTP (push-protocol endpoints plus /targets, /config, /-/reload,
       /api/v1/status/... debug surface) until a termination signal arrives
    8. Shut down in the reverse of steps 4-6

